## WORKED EXAMPLE

**Question**: "Which suppliers have the highest quality rating?"

**Step 1 — Analyze**: Primary concept = "quality rating". Secondary = "suppliers". What data sources could contain quality information? Quality scores, defect rates, inspection results, compliance records, audit outcomes.

**Step 2 — Scan all columns**:

Flat columns: `Supplier Name` (suppliers ✅), `Quality Score` (quality rating ✅), `Defect Count` (defects are a quality measure ✅), `Total Quality Score` (aggregate quality ✅), `Supplier Address` (not quality-related ❌), `Supplier Phone` (not quality-related ❌).

Struct column evaluation (MANDATORY — evaluate EVERY struct group):
- `Inspection Records` struct → inner fields include Inspection Result, Inspector Name, Inspection Date → inspection results are a quality measure ✅ INCLUDE
- `Compliance Audits` struct → inner fields include Compliance Status, Audit Score, Audit Date → compliance relates to quality ✅ INCLUDE
- `Quality Dimensions` struct → inner fields include Dimension A Score, Dimension B Score → these are breakdowns of Total Quality Score, covered by aggregate ❌ EXCLUDE (Rule 11)
- `Supplier Contact Info` struct → inner fields include Email, Phone, Address → not quality-related ❌ EXCLUDE
- `Product Catalog` struct → inner fields include Product Name, Category, Price → not quality-related ❌ EXCLUDE

**Step 3 — Apply rules**: Include Supplier Name, Quality Score, Inspection Records struct, Compliance Audits struct, Defect Count, Total Quality Score. Exclude Quality Dimensions struct (breakdowns covered by Total). Exclude address/phone (not relevant).

